    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d166-a1">

                <pb facs="0272" n="190" sameAs="frus:frus1925v02/pg190"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure—Translation<anchor n="85"
                            xml:id="d166-f1"/>]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The German Foreign Office to the American
                    Embassy</hi></head>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#superscript">No.V Steu 30</hi>/B 2556</p>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Note Verbale</hi></p>
                <p>The Foreign Office has the honor to inform the Embassy of the United States of
                    America, in reply to the latter’s <hi rendition="#italic">note verbale</hi> of
                        <pb facs="0273" n="191" xml:id="pg191"/> October 27, 1923 (No. 543),<anchor
                        n="86" xml:id="d166-f2"/> and supplementing its own <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >note verbale</hi> of September 5, 1923 (V Steu 1496),<anchor n="87"
                        xml:id="d166-f3"/> that, by an ordinance dated January 5, 1924,<anchor
                        n="88" xml:id="d166-f4"/> the Federal Minister of Finance has instructed the
                    competent financial authorities that incomes derived from the operation of ships
                    by citizens of the United States of America (individual persons) who have no
                    residence in Germany are likewise to be exempted from the income tax, under the
                    condition of reciprocity and the reservation of repeal at any time, as has
                    already been ordered by a proclamation of August 10, 1923,<anchor n="89"
                        xml:id="d166-f5"/> relating to American commercial companies as affected by
                    the corporation tax.</p>
                <p>Furthermore, according to the investigations undertaken by the German Government,
                    citizens of the United States who have no residence in Germany, as well as
                    American shipping companies which receive their incomes from the operation of
                    ships, have not been subjected in Germany to either the income or corporation
                    tax since January 1, 1921.</p>
                <p>The Foreign Office would be grateful for a statement as to whether now the
                    Government of the United States of America will grant to German shipping
                    companies and individual persons engaged in shipping the same exemption from
                    taxation of incomes derived from the operation of ships, and particularly so
                    with retroactive effect from January 1, 1921.</p>
                <closer><dateline rendition="#float-left"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-01-19"><hi rendition="#italic">January 19,
                        1924</hi></date>.</dateline></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
